<div class="max-w-4xl">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl mb-6">Events</h1>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6"><code>"on": &lbrace;"click": "toggle"&rbrace;</code> names a server event. When it fires, the handler's <code>params</code> carry the node id, the event kind, its payload, and &mdash; because the server kept the tree &mdash; the node's <code>props</code>. Nothing a client sends is trusted: three things are checked before a handler runs &mdash; the node exists in the tree this server last sent, it carries a handler of that kind naming that name, and the payload is the shape the kind declares. An event that fails any of them is <strong>dropped</strong>, not an error and not the end of the session: an event may arrive from a client one render behind, naming a node that existed a moment ago, and closing the session there would cost somebody their application for a mouse movement &mdash; while making one malformed frame an attack on every reader. <code>EUI_TRACE=1</code> prints what was dropped and why.</p>

    <h2 id="kinds" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Every event kind</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">30 of them. A kind the view has not declared cannot arrive.</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-12">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead><tr class="border-b border-white/10"><th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Kind</th><th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Fires when</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">click</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">A press and release on the node.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">double_click</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">Two clicks inside the double-click interval.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">pointer_down</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">The pointer went down on the node.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">pointer_up</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">And came back up.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">pointer_move</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">The pointer moved while over the node.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">pointer_enter</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">The pointer entered the node.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">pointer_leave</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">The pointer left it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">key_down</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">A key went down while the node had focus.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">key_up</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">And came back up.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">text_input</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">Text was typed or composed into the node.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">focus</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">The node took keyboard focus.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">blur</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">It lost focus.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">change</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">A field's value settled.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">submit</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">A form-shaped group was submitted.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">midi</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">The person played their MIDI instrument. See below.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">scroll</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">The node was scrolled.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">resize</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">The node's box changed size.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">context_menu</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">The secondary button, or its platform equivalent.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">drag_start</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">A drag began on the node.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">drag_over</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">A drag passed over it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">drop</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">A drag was released on it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">long_press</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">A press held past the platform threshold.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">window</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">The window itself changed &mdash; moved, resized, closed.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">ended</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">Media finished playing.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">time_update</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">Media playback advanced.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">wake</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">The session's own clock fired.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">file_pick</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">A file dialog returned a choice.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">file_save</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">A save dialog returned a destination.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">location</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">A position fix from the device. Phone only.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">nfc_tag</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">An NFC tag came within range. Phone only.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6"><code class="text-cyan-400">file_pick</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">file_save</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">location</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">nfc_tag</code> are answered by the client, and the last two are compiled only for a phone: a desktop has no tag reader and no positioning it can reach.</p>

    <h3 id="coalesced-events" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Coalesced events</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6"><code class="text-cyan-400">pointer_move</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">scroll</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">resize</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">drag_over</code> carry the latest value: the client sends at most one per frame per node. The server folds them once more against what has already arrived: an unbroken run of the same kind on the same node and handler, waiting behind a handler that is still running, is handled once, with the last value. Nothing else is folded or reordered. A handler slower than a frame &mdash; a score editor redrawing a page under a dragged note &mdash; therefore follows the hand rather than replaying a second of queued moves after the release.</p>

    <h3 id="midi-instruments" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">MIDI instruments</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">A node holding a <code class="text-cyan-400">midi</code> handler hears the person&rsquo;s MIDI keyboard while the application has the <code class="text-cyan-400">midi</code> capability (<code class="text-cyan-400">eui_capabilities("midi")</code> in <code class="text-cyan-400">config/routes.sl</code>, granted by the person) and its window is in front. The event&rsquo;s value is a list of <code class="text-cyan-400">[t_ms, status, data1, data2]</code> &mdash; every channel message since the last event, in order, never coalesced. System messages (SysEx included) never leave the client. On an <code class="text-cyan-400">audio</code> node that is playing, <code class="text-cyan-400">t_ms</code> is the sound&rsquo;s position, so a recording lines up with the backing track. An application that asks for <code class="text-cyan-400">midi</code> requires protocol 9 at the handshake (EUI 06 &sect;1.4).</p>

    <h3 id="answering-a-save" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Answering a save</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">A node that offers a file carries <code class="text-cyan-400">"save": "name.ext"</code> and a handler for <code class="text-cyan-400">file_save</code>. Pressing it opens the platform&rsquo;s save dialog; when the person has chosen a place, the handler runs and answers with the file, in the wrapped form &mdash; the new state, and the bytes:</p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6"><div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto"><pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># the node: &lbrace;"p": &lbrace;"save": "tune.mid"&rbrace;, "on": &lbrace;"file_save": "midi_save"&rbrace;&rbrace;
"midi_save" =&gt; &lbrace;"state": state, "save": score_midi(state["src"])&rbrace;,</code></pre></div></div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6"><code class="text-cyan-400">save</code> is a string (written as its UTF-8) or a list of bytes, each 0 to 255. The server sends it to that node as <code class="text-cyan-400">Blob</code> frames, in chunks of at most 256 KiB, and the client writes it where the person chose; anything else aborts the save with the reason. The key is read only for a <code class="text-cyan-400">file_save</code>, and only in the wrapped form: a bare state that happens to hold a <code class="text-cyan-400">save</code> key is still a state.</p>

    <h2 id="waking" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Waking a session</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">A component may ask to be woken on a clock &mdash; the <code class="text-cyan-400">wake</code> event. On its own that makes every window a poller: it learns what someone else did only when its own clock next fires, and spends a render per period discovering that nothing happened.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6"><code class="text-cyan-400">eui_wake(component)</code> is the other half &mdash; it renders every <em>other</em> live session of a component immediately, from wherever the change was written.</p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6"><div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto"><pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">def send(state, params)
  Message.create(&lbrace; "room": state["room"], "body": params["value"] &rbrace;)
  eui_wake("chat")        # every other window showing this component, now
  state
end</code></pre></div></div>
</div>
